# Programs whose TRACE setting is not the one the body was first entered
# under, which is the axis a compiled-in emission decision can get wrong and
# no other case table exercises.
#
# The setting decides which trace ops a body compiles to, so every row here
# is a place where the setting in force when a clause runs can differ from
# the setting the chunk carrying that clause was compiled under. Two ways
# that happens, and both directions of each: a TRACE executed inside the
# body after the chunk exists, and the same body entered again under a
# different setting.
#
# These are boundary cases, not shapes. A promoted clause is where the echo
# is decided, so each row puts the setting change immediately before one --
# an IF header, a SELECT header, a listed WHEN -- rather than before an
# ordinary clause, which the tree-walker's own gate answers whatever the
# compiler does.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE, and every
# row passed with nothing promoted, which is what says a row can tell
# whether it would ever have failed. Regenerate a row by running its program
# under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 -- ir_recorded.rs refuses to run at all
# under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty: an
# empty line ends an expected block, and a program's own output may hold
# one. Trailing blanks are part of what is compared -- an IF header's *-*
# echo ends in one -- and are preserved verbatim by both this format and the
# comparison.

# trace turned on inside a branch, before a later if
# The chunk is compiled untraced -- the body's own TRACE runs after it
# exists -- so the second IF, a promoted clause of that same chunk, has to
# echo anyway.
program
if 1 = 1 then trace r
if 1 = 1 then say 'x'
----
rc> 0
out> x
err>      2 *-* if 1 = 1 
err>        >>>   "1"
err>      2 *-*   then
err>      2 *-*     say 'x'
err>        >>>       "x"

# trace turned off inside a branch, before a later if
# The other direction, which a fallback that only ever *adds* an echo would
# pass: the second IF must print nothing at all.
program
trace r
if 1 = 1 then trace n
if 1 = 1 then say 'x'
----
rc> 0
out> x
err>      2 *-* if 1 = 1 
err>        >>>   "1"
err>      2 *-*   then
err>      2 *-*     trace n

# trace turned on inside a loop body, before a later if
# Inside a loop body, so the change has to reach the later passes as well as
# the rest of the pass that made it. The loop's own per-pass re-echo of DO
# and END is the surrounding evidence that it did.
program
do i = 1 to 2
  if i = 1 then trace r
  if i = 2 then say 'two'
end
----
rc> 0
out> two
err>      3 *-*   if i = 2 
err>        >>>     "0"
err>      4 *-* end
err>      1 *-* do i = 1 to 2
err>        >>>     "1"
err>        >>>     "2"
err>      2 *-*   if i = 1 
err>        >>>     "0"
err>      3 *-*   if i = 2 
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      3 *-*     then
err>      3 *-*       say 'two'
err>        >>>         "two"
err>      4 *-* end
err>      1 *-* do i = 1 to 2
err>        >>>     "2"
err>        >>>     "3"

# one body entered untraced and then under trace r
# The same body entered twice, untraced and then traced, in one process. A
# chunk cache that hands back whichever chunk was compiled first is what
# this row is about.
program
call sub
trace r
call sub
exit
sub:
if 1 = 1 then say 'in'
return
----
rc> 0
out> in
out> in
err>      3 *-* call sub
err>      5 *-*   sub:
err>      6 *-*   if 1 = 1 
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      6 *-*     then
err>      6 *-*       say 'in'
err>        >>>         "in"
err>      7 *-*   return
err>      4 *-* exit

# a body entered under trace r that then turns trace off
# The row that reaches a chunk compiled to echo and then asks it not to.
# Every row whose TRACE is in the body itself compiles that body before the
# TRACE runs, so the chunk is the silent one; here the body is *entered*
# with trace r in force, so its chunk carries an echo op per promoted
# clause and the trace n inside it has to withdraw them.
#
# The `3 *-* exit` line is the surrounding evidence that the withdrawal is
# per activation: trace n ran in the callee's, and the caller's own next
# clause echoes.
program
trace r
call sub
exit
sub:
if 1 = 1 then trace n
if 1 = 1 then say 'x'
return
----
rc> 0
out> x
err>      2 *-* call sub
err>      4 *-*   sub:
err>      5 *-*   if 1 = 1 
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      5 *-*     then
err>      5 *-*       trace n
err>      3 *-* exit

# trace turned on inside a branch, before a select
# A SELECT's header and its listed WHEN are promoted clauses too, and each
# has an echo of its own -- so the row that says the fix is about clauses
# rather than about IF.
program
if 1 = 1 then trace r
select
  when 1 = 0 then say 'a'
  otherwise say 'o'
end
----
rc> 0
out> o
err>      2 *-* select
err>      3 *-*   when 1 = 0 
err>        >>>     "0"
err>      4 *-*   otherwise
err>      4 *-*     say 'o'
err>        >>>       "o"
err>      5 *-* end

# trace i over a literal in an assignment
# The one row that is a guard rather than a regression test. It has no
# promoted clause at all: an assignment is Generic today, so both engines
# route the whole clause through eval.rs and the >L> line comes out of
# evaluating the literal. The spike promoted this assignment and dropped
# that line, because a native constant op evaluates nothing. This row goes
# red when the first native expression op forgets to re-emit it.
program
trace i
n1 = 'lit'
say n1
----
rc> 0
out> lit
err>      2 *-* n1 = 'lit'
err>        >L>   "lit"
err>        >>>   "lit"
err>        >=>   N1 <= "lit"
err>      3 *-* say n1
err>        >V>   N1 => "lit"
err>        >>>   "lit"

# trace turned on by CALL TRACE, not by the TRACE instruction
# The setting a body reaches through the `TRACE()` builtin rather than
# through its own TRACE instruction, which is the route `Plan::never_retraces`
# exists to see: the builtin sets the *running* activation's setting, so a
# chunk that had left its value echoes out would have none to turn on. The
# `>L>`, `>V>` and `>O>` lines below are what a chunk without them loses,
# and the `>A>` line is the builtin's own argument.
program
zs = 'ab'
if 1 = 1 then call trace 'i'
if 2 = 2 then say zs
call trace 'o'
say 'done'
----
rc> 0
out> ab
out> done
err>        >>>       "N"
err>      3 *-* if 2 = 2 
err>        >L>   "2"
err>        >L>   "2"
err>        >O>   "=" => "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>      3 *-*   then
err>      3 *-*     say zs
err>        >V>       ZS => "ab"
err>        >>>       "ab"
err>      4 *-* call trace 'o'
err>        >L>   "o"
err>        >A>   "o"

# an assignment whose own expression turns tracing on
# The oracle decides an assignment's `>>>` before it evaluates:
# `RexxInstructionAssignment::execute` branches on `tracingInstructions()`
# and its fast path never calls `traceResult`, so a `TRACE` reached inside
# the expression cannot bring the line back. `>F>` and `>=>` still print --
# the call machinery and `assign` each read the setting where they run.
program
zs = 'ab'
zg = trace('i')
say zs
----
rc> 0
out> ab
err>        >F>   TRACE => "N"
err>        >=>   ZG <= "N"
err>      3 *-* say zs
err>        >V>   ZS => "ab"
err>        >>>   "ab"

# the same, with the expression carrying on after the setting changed
# `>F>`, `>L>` and `>O>` all print and `>>>` still does not, which is what
# says the missing line is the instruction's own decision and not a gate on
# the value.
program
/* assignment whose expression turns it on and then does more work */
zg = trace('i') || 'q'
say 'after'
----
rc> 0
out> after
err>        >F>   TRACE => "N"
err>        >L>   "q"
err>        >O>   "||" => "Nq"
err>        >=>   ZG <= "Nq"
err>      3 *-* say 'after'
err>        >L>   "after"
err>        >>>   "after"

# SAY, whose instruction re-reads the setting
# The adjacent success: `tracingInstructions()` appears in exactly one file
# under `interpreter/instructions/`, so every other instruction echoes what
# the setting says when the echo runs. A fix applied to `trace_result`
# itself rather than to the assignment would drop this row's `>>>`.
program
/* SAY: does it re-read the setting mid-clause? */
say trace('i')
say 'after'
----
rc> 0
out> N
out> after
err>        >F>   TRACE => "N"
err>        >>>   "N"
err>      3 *-* say 'after'
err>        >L>   "after"
err>        >>>   "after"

# traced at entry, turned off inside the expression
# The other direction, and it needs no entry state: `traceResult` and the
# assignment echo both read the setting in force when they run, so a clause
# that switches tracing off part way owes neither line.
program
/* the reverse: on at entry, turned off inside the expression */
trace i
zg = trace('o')
say 'after'
----
rc> 0
out> after
err>      3 *-* zg = trace('o')
err>        >L>   "o"
err>        >A>   "o"

# a callee that changes the setting, with the caller's clause resuming
# The caller is traced at entry and the routine turns tracing off inside its
# own activation. The setting comes back with the return, and so must the
# entry state the caller's clause recorded -- `save_clause_state` is what
# carries it. Dropping this field from that copy reddens this row.
program
/* the caller is traced, the callee turns it off internally, caller resumes */
trace i
zg = sub() || 'q'
say 'after'
exit
sub:
  trace o
  return 'S'
----
rc> 0
out> after
err>      3 *-* zg = sub() || 'q'
err>      6 *-*   sub:
err>      7 *-*   trace o
err>        >F>   SUB => "S"
err>        >L>   "q"
err>        >O>   "||" => "Sq"
err>        >>>   "Sq"
err>        >=>   ZG <= "Sq"
err>      4 *-* say 'after'
err>        >L>   "after"
err>        >>>   "after"
err>      5 *-* exit
